{% extends 'help/_layout.html.twig' %}
{% block help_title %}Importing Health Data{% endblock %}

{% block help_body %}
<figure class="figure d-block border rounded shadow-sm overflow-hidden mb-4 w-100">
    <img src="{{ asset('images/help/health-metrics.png') }}" class="figure-img img-fluid mb-0 w-100"
         alt="The Health Metrics page showing weight, glucose, heart rate, steps, and sleep">
</figure>
<p>NouriLoop becomes much more useful when it can see your body's numbers alongside your food. You can bring in
weight, blood glucose, blood pressure, heart rate, body fat, steps, and sleep.</p>

<h2 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">Importing a file</h2>
<ol>
    <li>Go to <strong>Health Metrics → Import</strong>.</li>
    <li>Upload an <strong>Apple Health export</strong>, a <strong>health-export JSON</strong>, or a
        <strong>CSV</strong>. NouriLoop detects the format automatically.</li>
</ol>

<h2 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">Automatic syncing (recommended for large histories)</h2>
<p>A full Apple Health export is too big to upload directly. For ongoing, hands-off syncing, create an
<strong>API token</strong> in Settings and point an app like <em>Health Auto Export</em> at NouriLoop — it
posts your readings on a schedule. <a href="{{ path('app_help_show', {topic: 'settings'}) }}">See Settings →</a></p>
<p>Connecting a <strong>Lingo or Stelo</strong> glucose sensor? There's a dedicated walkthrough:
<a href="{{ path('app_help_show', {topic: 'connect-cgm'}) }}">Connect Your Lingo or Stelo</a>.</p>

<h2 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">Reading the page</h2>
<p>Each metric gets a card with a trend chart. The sublabel tells you what the number is: <strong>daily
totals</strong> for steps, <strong>daily averages</strong> (with a shaded range) for glucose, heart rate, and
blood pressure, and <strong>daily readings</strong> for weight and body fat.</p>

<p class="text-muted small">Your health data is handled carefully — see the Health Data Privacy Notice linked
on the Health Metrics page.</p>
{% endblock %}

